tructures together — every member of your group sees the same live map. Creating a team world will be a Supporter perk; joining will be free for everyone you invite. It is not ready yet — get a heads-up when it launches.\",\"notifyCta\":\"Notify me at launch\",\"notifySignedOut\":\"Sign in to get notified\",\"notifyDone\":\"You're on the list — we'll email you at launch.\",\"notifyError\":\"Couldn't save that — please try again.\",\"maybeLater\":\"Maybe later\"},\"shell\":{\"boot\":{\"offline\":\"You are offline — the map cannot load new ground\",\"paneOffline\":\"You are offline, and this panel has not downloaded yet. It opens once you are back online.\",\"oldTitle\":\"This browser is too old for the map\",\"oldBody\":\"The seed map needs a recent browser. Update your browser, or open this page in Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari.\",\"engineTitle\":\"The map engine did not start\",\"engineBody\":\"Something blocked the download of the map engine. A reload usually fixes it. If it keeps happening, check an ad-blocker or a strict firewall.\",\"gpuTitle\":\"Your graphics could not start\",\"gpuBody\":\"The map draws with your graphics card and it said no. Try turning on hardware acceleration in your browser settings, then reload.\",\"slowTitle\":\"This is taking longer than usual\",\"slowBody\":\"The map is still loading. On a slow connection the first visit can take a minute; after that it is instant.\",\"reload\":\"Reload\",\"keepWaiting\":\"Keep waiting\",\"slowLink\":\"Still loading. The first visit is the slow one — after it, the map starts much faster.\",\"simpleLine\":\"Simple map — your browser could not start its graphics, so the map may move less smoothly. Nothing is missing.\",\"tryFull\":\"Try the full map\"},\"share\":{\"kicker\":\"Share this map\",\"linkField\":\"The link to this map\",\"copyFor\":\"Copy for\",\"videoDescription\":\"Video description\",\"reddit\":\"Reddit\",\"seedOnly\":\"Seed only\",\"imageKicker\":\"Image of the map\",\"pictureKicker\":\"Picture\",\"pinsKicker\":\"Pins\",\"layoutGroup\":\"Picture shape\",\"downloadPng\":\"Download PNG\",\"savePicture\":\"Save picture\",\"savedTick\":\"Saved ✓\",\"captionPlain\":\"What you see now, with the markers.\",\"captionBand\":\"What you see now, with the markers and the seed on the band.\",\"captionCorner\":\"What you see now, with the markers and the seed in the corner.\",\"exportKicker\":\"Export my pins\",\"placeSharing\":\"Sharing {name}\",\"placeHint\":\"the link opens the map right here\"},\"file\":{\"kicker\":\"From a world file\",\"drop\":\"\u003cb\u003eDrop a level.dat here\u003c/b\u003e or \u003cchoose\u003echoose a file\u003c/choose\u003e\",\"note\":\"Reads the seed and version from a Java world. Nothing is uploaded.\",\"release\":\"Let go to read it\",\"reading\":\"Reading the file…\",\"loaded\":\"Loaded seed {seed}\",\"fromLevelDat\":\"This seed came from your level.dat\"},\"export\":{\"kicker\":\"Export options\",\"formatTip\":\"Save your {count, plural, one {# pin} other {# pins}} as {format}\",\"empty\":\"No pins to export yet.\",\"addOne\":\"Add one\",\"button\":\"Export\",\"buttonTip\":\"Save your {count, plural, one {# pin} other {# pins}} to a file\",\"buttonTipEmpty\":\"No pins to export yet — open it to import pins from a file\",\"done\":\"{count, plural, one {# pin exported} other {# pins exported}}\",\"formatNote\":\"JSON and GeoJSON keep everything, notes and the world included. CSV keeps each pin but not the seed, edition or version.\",\"modXaero\":\"Xaero's Minimap\",\"modJourneyMap\":\"JourneyMap\",\"modNote\":\"Xaero's Minimap and JourneyMap keep each pin's name, place and colour (Xaero's takes the nearest of its own colours), not its note.\",\"howXaero\":\"In the game, leave the world. In .minecraft/xaero/minimap, open your world's folder (Multiplayer_ and the address, for a server), then {folder}. Paste the file's lines at the end of the .txt file there, then join again.\",\"howJourneyMap\":\"In the game, leave the world. Put the file in your world's waypoints folder, in .minecraft/journeymap/data/sp (data/mp for a server). Join again: JourneyMap adds these pins to yours.\"},\"notices\":{\"zoomHint\":\"Zoom in to show \u003cicons\u003e\u003c/icons\u003e\"},\"page\":{\"title\":\"Minecraft seed map for Java and Bedrock\",\"fresh\":\"Current for Java {java} and Bedrock {bedrock}\",\"checked\":\"Checked {date}\",\"lede\":\"Type your seed in the box at the top and the map draws your world before you set foot in it: the biomes, and where the villages, strongholds, ancient cities and monuments are waiting. Click a marker for its X and Z and a \u003ccode\u003e/tp\u003c/code\u003e line you can paste straight into chat.\",\"factEditions\":\"Java, Large Biomes and Bedrock, in the Overworld, the Nether and the End\",\"factVersions\":\"Every Java version back to {java}, and Bedrock back to {bedrock}\",\"factMarkers\":\"Every marker says how often it is really there, for that structure and your edition\",\"factLanguages\":\"{count} languages, each with a page of its own\",\"factLocal\":\"It all happens in your browser; sign in and your saved seeds and pins sync to your account\",\"accTitle\":\"How accurate is this seed map?\",\"accLede\":\"Open a marker and it tells you how often that thing really stands where the map puts it, up to \u003cstrong\u003e{accuracy}%\u003c/strong\u003e, for that structure in your edition. Villages, strongholds and ocean monuments are the safest bets. A few things that move about with the ground around them say Estimate instead of a number.\",\"accNote\":\"\u003cstrong\u003eEstimate\u003c/strong\u003e means the map will point you at the spot but will not put a number on it. Ravines, mineshafts and caves move about with the ground, so go and look, and dig a little either side of the marker. On Bedrock a few of the numbers sit a bit lower than on Java for the same seed.\",\"accLink\":\"How accurate are Minecraft seed maps?\",\"accLinkCaption\":\"how good it is, feature by feature\",\"stepSeed\":\"Enter your seed\",\"stepPick\":\"Pick edition, version and dimension\",\"stepPickBody\":\"Java, Large Biomes or Bedrock, then the exact version your world was made in, then Overworld, Nether or End. Minecraft changed the shape of its worlds at 1.13, 1.15 and 1.18, so a 1.12 world and a 1.18 world from the same seed look nothing alike.\",\"stepControls\":\"Mouse, keyboard and touch\",\"kickMouse\":\"Mouse\",\"kickKeyboard\":\"Keyboard\",\"kickTouch\":\"Touch\",\"keySlash\":\"\u003cstrong\u003e/\u003c/strong\u003e jumps to the seed box\",\"stepSave\":\"Save what you find\",\"stepSaveBody\":\"Your pins are your own: drop one anywhere, give it a name, and it waits in My pins. Ticking off is for what the map found for you: press Tick off on a structure or an ore and its marker greys out and moves to Ticked off. Both stay in this browser along with your view, so the map opens where you left off. Share the link with a friend and they get the same seed, dimension, place and zoom.\",\"strTitle\":\"Find a structure\",\"strLede\":\"Light a structure up on the row under the map and every one of them shows, with how far it is from where you are standing. Each one has a finder page of its own too, if that is the only thing you are hunting.\",\"colFinderPage\":\"Its own finder page\",\"onMap\":\"on the map\",\"slimeNote\":\"Slime chunks are a layer, not a structure. Switch it on and the map shades every chunk where slimes can spawn. Caves, veins and buried treasure stay hidden until you zoom right in.\",\"finderLabel\":\"{feature} Finder\",\"bioTitle\":\"Find a biome\",\"bioLede\":\"Pick a biome and everything else fades back, so a cherry grove or a mushroom island stands out from a long way off. The list follows your edition. Choose Bedrock and it uses Bedrock’s names, and the search box takes either one. For cave biomes, set the biome height to Underground first.\",\"bioAll\":\"Minecraft Biome Finder, all {count}+ biomes\",\"coordTitle\":\"Coordinates, the Nether and the world border\",\"readHeading\":\"Reading coordinates\",\"readBody\":\"The line under the map follows your pointer: \u003cstrong\u003eX\u003c/strong\u003e across, \u003cstrong\u003eZ\u003c/strong\u003e up and down, and the biome you are over. In game, press F3 on Java, or switch coordinates on in Bedrock’s world settings, and you get the same three numbers. Open a marker and it hands you the exact X and Z plus a \u003ccode\u003e/tp\u003c/code\u003e line for chat.\",\"netherHeading\":\"Overworld to Nether: divide by eight\",\"netherBody\":\"One block in the Nether is eight in the Overworld. Divide your Overworld X and Z by 8 and that is where the other portal goes. Y stays the same. The map prints the matching Nether coordinate beside every one it hands you, so you never have to do the sum yourself.\",\"coordsExample\":\"X {x}   Z {z}\",\"borderHeading\":\"Drawing a world border\",\"borderBody\":\"Switch the border on in Settings, set the middle and the radius, and the map draws the bit of world you can play in. A square, the shape Java’s \u003ccode\u003e/worldborder\u003c/code\u003e makes, or a circle. It counts the blocks across as you move around, and the copy button hands you both commands. Bedrock has no \u003ccode\u003e/worldborder\u003c/code\u003e, so there the border is for planning only.\",\"oreTitle\":\"Minecraft Ore Finder\",\"oreLede\":\"Tick an ore and the map lights up the chunks where it sits thickest, from the same seed the map is already drawing. One ore at a time, and each one carries its own number for the edition you picked.\",\"colDig\":\"Dig around\",\"javaOnly\":\"Java only\",\"oreQ1\":\"How does the Ore Finder work?\",\"oreA1\":\"Tick the ore you want, set a Y range if you already know where you want to dig, and the map lights up the hotspots. A hotspot is a chunk where that ore sits thicker than usual, at the Y shown beside it. Head there, dig down to that Y and branch out a few blocks each way. Coal is Java only, diamond hotspots are a Supporter perk, and older versions hide their ore at other heights.\",\"oreQ2\":\"Why is it not always right?\",\"oreA2\":\"The number beside each ore is how often a hotspot pays off. Most of them will have the ore waiting for you. A few will not, and a vein can sit a few blocks from the middle of the chunk, so dig around a bit before you move on to the next hotspot. Bedrock and older versions are a little less exact.\",\"faqWhatQ\":\"What is a Minecraft seed map?\",\"faqWhatA\":\"It draws the world a Minecraft seed will make, before you ever load it: the biomes, and where the villages, strongholds, ancient cities and monuments will stand. Type the seed, pick your edition and version, and you get coordinates you can walk to. Nothing is downloaded; sign in and your saved seeds and pins sync to your account.\",\"faqAccQ\":\"Is this seed map accurate?\",\"faqAccA\":\"Open a marker and it tells you how often that thing really stands there, up to \u003cstrong\u003e{accuracy}%\u003c/strong\u003e, for that structure in your edition. Villages, strongholds and monuments are the safest bets. A few things that move about with the ground around them say Estimate instead of a number. On Bedrock the numbers can sit a bit lower than on Java for the same seed.\",\"faqSeedA\":\"In a Java single-player world, type \u003ccode\u003e/seed\u003c/code\u003e in chat; it works with cheats off. On Bedrock, read it off your world's settings screen, no cheats needed. On a server or Realm, ask the owner for it. If you have the save folder, drop the Java \u003ccode\u003elevel.dat\u003c/code\u003e on this page and the seed and version fill themselves in.\",\"faqVersionsQ\":\"Which Minecraft versions does the map cover?\",\"faqVersionsA\":\"Java from {javaMin} up to {java}, Bedrock from {bedrockMin} up to {bedrock}, and Large Biomes on top. Pick the exact version your world was made in. Minecraft changed the shape of its worlds at 1.13, 1.15 and 1.18, so a 1.12 world and a 1.18 world from the same seed look nothing alike.\",\"faqNetherQ\":\"How do I turn Overworld coordinates into Nether coordinates?\",\"faqNetherA\":\"Divide by eight. One block in the Nether is eight in the Overworld, so Overworld X {owX} and Z {owZ} is Nether X {neX} and Z {neZ}, and Y stays the same. The map prints the matching coordinate beside every one it hands you, so you never have to do the sum yourself.\",\"faqKeepQ\":\"How do I keep a seed so I can come back to it?\",\"faqKeepA\":\"Press save on the toolbar, next to Share. The world you are looking at joins your seeds, with its edition and version. Press again to drop it. Signed out, they stay in this browser. Sign in with a free account and they follow you to every device.\",\"faqSlimeQ\":\"How do I find slime chunks?\",\"faqSlimeA\":\"Switch on the slime-chunk layer and the map shades every chunk where slimes can spawn in the Overworld. Slime chunks come from the seed and the chunk, not from the biome. Below Y 40 they spawn at any light level. Swamps are the other place, and there it goes by light and moon phase.\",\"faqStrongholdQ\":\"How do I find a stronghold and the End portal?\",\"faqStrongholdA\":\"Switch strongholds on and the map marks all of them. Open a marker for its coordinates and, where it is known, the End portal itself and how many eyes are already in the frame. Copy the \u003ccode\u003e/tp\u003c/code\u003e line from the marker. Strongholds sit deep, so expect to dig well below sea level.\",\"faqSyncQ\":\"Do my pins and kept seeds sync across devices?\",\"faqSyncA\":\"On their own they stay in this browser. Make a free account, sign in, and your seeds, pins and ticked-off structures follow you to every device. The map works the same either way.\",\"faqCheatA\":\"That is up to how you play. For exploring your own world, or hunting a biome for a build, most people are happy with it. On a server, read the rules first, because some do not allow seed tools.\",\"bioNamesLede\":\"Bedrock still calls a fair few biomes by their old names. Pick Bedrock above the map and the biome list swaps over on its own, and the search box takes either name. Here is the whole lot.\",\"troubleTitle\":\"Map not matching your world?\",\"troubleLede\":\"When the map and your world disagree, it is nearly always one of these six.\",\"toolsTitle\":\"More Minecraft tools\",\"toolItems\":\"Items, blocks and mobs\",\"readNext\":\"Read next\",\"readUse\":\"How to use a seed map\",\"readSeed\":\"How to find your Minecraft seed\",\"readDiamonds\":\"How to find diamonds in Minecraft\"},\"supporter\":{\"title\":\"Become a Supporter\",\"body\":\"No ads, the Diamond Finder, and your pins and seeds on every device. You also keep the map free for everyone else.\",\"cta\":\"See the perks\",\"priceMonth\":\"From {price} a month\",\"price\":\"From {price}\",\"rowTitle\":\"Supporter\",\"rowSub\":\"Every ore cluster, on every seed\",\"rowCta\":\"Join\",\"thanks\":\"You're a Supporter — thank you for keeping the site running.\",\"nudgeTitle\":\"Find diamonds faster.\",\"nudgeSub\":\"Every diamond cluster on your seed, from {price} a month.\",\"nudgeSubPass\":\"Every diamond cluster on your seed, from {price}.\",\"notNow\":\"Not now\"},\"ad\":{\"label\":\"Advertisement\",\"adFree\":\"Ad-free with Supporter\"},\"settings\":{\"sections\":{\"onTheMap\":\"On the map\",\"readingMap\":\"Reading the map\",\"terrain\":\"Terrain\",\"markersPins\":\"Markers and pins\",\"worldBorder\":\"World border\",\"account\":\"Account\",\"thisDevice\":\"This device\",\"keyboard\":\"Keyboard\"},\"shareMap\":\"Share this map\",\"readout\":\"Cursor readout\",\"readoutSub\":\"X, Z and the biome under the mouse, on the line above the bottom bar\",\"chunkInReadout\":\"Chunk in the readout\",\"tapMark\":\"Mark where you tap\",\"readoutAtCursor\":\"Readout at the cursor\",\"readoutAtCursorSub\":\"Coordinates and biome follow the mouse\",\"spawnSub\":\"Shortcut: H\",\"biomesAtHeightSub\":\"Caves change the biome below the surface\",\"heightLine\"