 where you want to dig, and the map lights up the hotspots. A hotspot is a chunk where that ore sits thicker than usual, at the Y shown beside it. Head there, dig down to that Y and branch out a few blocks each way. Coal is Java only, diamond hotspots are a Supporter perk, and older versions hide their ore at other heights.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Why is it not always right?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"The number beside each ore is how often a hotspot pays off. Most of them will have the ore waiting for you. A few will not, and a vein can sit a few blocks from the middle of the chunk, so dig around a bit before you move on to the next hotspot. Bedrock and older versions are a little less exact.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Which ores can the finder locate?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"In the Overworld it finds diamond, iron, copper, gold, redstone, lapis lazuli and coal, and in the Nether it finds ancient debris (netherite). You search one ore at a time and can optionally limit the search to a specific Y level.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"What is the best Y level to mine diamonds?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Diamonds get more common the deeper you dig, peaking around Y -59 in current versions (they generate between Y -64 and Y 16). The finder defaults to each ore's full spawn range, and you can tick 'limit to a specific Y level' to focus on the richest band – for diamonds, around Y -59 to -54.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Does the Ore Finder work for my Minecraft version?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"It works on both Java and Bedrock Edition. On Java it covers modern versions, including 1.21 and the 26.x releases, which share the same ore-generation rules. Bedrock Edition is now supported as well – on 1.18 and newer it finds the Overworld ores (diamond, iron, copper, gold, redstone and lapis lazuli). The two editions use different world generators, so make sure you pick the edition your world was created in.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Is using the Ore Finder cheating?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"That is up to how you like to play. In single-player it is just a faster version of strip-mining for a diamond start. On multiplayer servers, check the rules first – some servers don't allow seed-based tools.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"What is a Minecraft seed map?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"It draws the world a Minecraft seed will make, before you ever load it: the biomes, and where the villages, strongholds, ancient cities and monuments will stand. Type the seed, pick your edition and version, and you get coordinates you can walk to. Nothing is downloaded; sign in and your saved seeds and pins sync to your account.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Is this seed map accurate?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Open a marker and it tells you how often that thing really stands there, up to 99%, for that structure in your edition. Villages, strongholds and monuments are the safest bets. A few things that move about with the ground around them say Estimate instead of a number. On Bedrock the numbers can sit a bit lower than on Java for the same seed.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How do I find the seed of my world?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"In a Java single-player world, type /seed in chat; it works with cheats off. On Bedrock, read it off your world's settings screen, no cheats needed. On a server or Realm, ask the owner for it. If you have the save folder, drop the Java level.dat on this page and the seed and version fill themselves in.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Are Java and Bedrock seeds the same?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"No. Java Edition and Bedrock Edition use different world-generation algorithms, so the same seed produces completely different worlds in each edition. Always pick the matching edition before generating the map.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Which Minecraft versions does the map cover?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Java from 1.0 up to 26.3, Bedrock from 1.16 up to 1.26.60, and Large Biomes on top. Pick the exact version your world was made in. Minecraft changed the shape of its worlds at 1.13, 1.15 and 1.18, so a 1.12 world and a 1.18 world from the same seed look nothing alike.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How do I turn Overworld coordinates into Nether coordinates?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Divide by eight. One block in the Nether is eight in the Overworld, so Overworld X 1,600 and Z 800 is Nether X 200 and Z 100, and Y stays the same. The map prints the matching coordinate beside every one it hands you, so you never have to do the sum yourself.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How do I keep a seed so I can come back to it?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Press save on the toolbar, next to Share. The world you are looking at joins your seeds, with its edition and version. Press again to drop it. Signed out, they stay in this browser. Sign in with a free account and they follow you to every device.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How do I find slime chunks?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Switch on the slime-chunk layer and the map shades every chunk where slimes can spawn in the Overworld. Slime chunks come from the seed and the chunk, not from the biome. Below Y 40 they spawn at any light level. Swamps are the other place, and there it goes by light and moon phase.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How do I find a stronghold and the End portal?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Switch strongholds on and the map marks all of them. Open a marker for its coordinates and, where it is known, the End portal itself and how many eyes are already in the frame. Copy the /tp line from the marker. Strongholds sit deep, so expect to dig well below sea level.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Why does the map show a structure that isn't in my world — and how do I fix it?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Almost always it is a version or edition mismatch. Java and Bedrock generate completely different worlds from the same seed, and even within one edition the structure layout changes across major updates. Check that the edition and the exact version selected above the map match the world you are playing, then regenerate. Some markers are chunk-centre approximations, so the real structure can sit a few blocks from the marker; and on rare occasions a marker fails to render, which reloading the page fixes.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Do my pins and kept seeds sync across devices?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"On their own they stay in this browser. Make a free account, sign in, and your seeds, pins and ticked-off structures follow you to every device. The map works the same either way.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Is using the Minecraft Seed Map considered cheating?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"That is up to how you play. For exploring your own world, or hunting a biome for a build, most people are happy with it. On a server, read the rules first, because some do not allow seed tools.\"}}]}cc:[\"$\",\"script\",null,{\"type\":\"application/ld+json\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"$11f\"}}]\ndf:[\"$\",\"$L121\",null,{}]\ne2:[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"ms-pb-nm\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$Le1\",null,{\"src\":\"/images/structures/trial_chambers.webp\",\"alt\":\"\",\"width\":32,\"height\":32,\"className\":\"object-contain\",\"style\":{\"width\":32,\"height\":32},\"unoptimized\":false,\"draggable\":\"$undefined\",\"priority\":false}],[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Trial