\":\"o2\",\"question\":\"Which ores can the finder locate?\",\"children\":\"In the Overworld it finds diamond, iron, copper, gold, redstone, lapis lazuli and coal, and in the Nether it finds ancient debris (netherite). You search one ore at a time and can optionally limit the search to a specific Y level.\"}],[\"$\",\"$L111\",\"o3\",{\"id\":\"o3\",\"question\":\"What is the best Y level to mine diamonds?\",\"children\":\"Diamonds get more common the deeper you dig, peaking around Y -59 in current versions (they generate between Y -64 and Y 16). The finder defaults to each ore's full spawn range, and you can tick 'limit to a specific Y level' to focus on the richest band – for diamonds, around Y -59 to -54.\"}],[\"$\",\"$L111\",\"o4\",{\"id\":\"o4\",\"question\":\"Does the Ore Finder work for my Minecraft version?\",\"children\":\"It works on both Java and Bedrock Edition. On Java it covers modern versions, including 1.21 and the 26.x releases, which share the same ore-generation rules. Bedrock Edition is now supported as well – on 1.18 and newer it finds the Overworld ores (diamond, iron, copper, gold, redstone and lapis lazuli). The two editions use different world generators, so make sure you pick the edition your world was created in.\"}],[\"$\",\"$L111\",\"o5\",{\"id\":\"o5\",\"question\":\"Is using the Ore Finder cheating?\",\"children\":\"That is up to how you like to play. In single-player it is just a faster version of strip-mining for a diamond start. On multiplayer servers, check the rules first – some servers don't allow seed-based tools.\"}]]}]\n110:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"ms-pb-frow\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$Lee\",null,{\"prefetch\":false,\"className\":\"ms-map-popup-link ms-pb-link\",\"href\":\"/tools/ore-finder\",\"children\":\"Minecraft Ore Finder\"}],[\"$\",\"$Lee\",null,{\"prefetch\":false,\"className\":\"ms-map-popup-link ms-pb-link\",\"href\":\"/tools/diamond-finder\",\"children\":\"Diamond Finder\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"classNam